I kept this version straightforward and checked capacities one by one. The first possible capacity must be the heaviest package. For each capacity, the code loads packages in order and counts how many days are needed.

This is slower than binary search, but it is easy to follow. It also shows the greedy check clearly. The input vector is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool canShip(vector<int>& weights, int days, int capacity) {
        int usedDays = 1;
        int currentLoad = 0;

        for (int weight : weights) {
            if (currentLoad + weight > capacity) {
                usedDays++;
                currentLoad = weight;
            } else {
                currentLoad += weight;
            }
        }

        return usedDays <= days;
    }

    int shipWithinDays(vector<int>& weights, int days) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = 0;

        for (int weight : weights) {
            low = max(low, weight);
            high += weight;
        }

        for (int capacity = low; capacity <= high; capacity++) {
            if (canShip(weights, days, capacity)) {
                return capacity;
            }
        }

        return high;
    }
};
```

For a single package, the loop starts at that package's weight and returns it immediately. The time complexity is `O(n * (sum(weights) - max(weights) + 1))`, and the extra space is `O(1)`.

This Java version uses a recursive binary search. The helper checks one possible capacity by filling each day as much as possible without changing the package order.

If the helper uses too many days, the capacity needs to increase. If it fits, the current value is saved and the search continues on the smaller side.

The edge cases are covered by the bounds: when `days == 1`, the answer becomes the total weight, and when the number of days is at least the number of packages, the answer is the heaviest package.

```java
class Solution {
    public int shipWithinDays(int[] weights, int days) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = 0;

        for (int weight : weights) {
            low = Math.max(low, weight);
            high += weight;
        }

        return search(weights, days, low, high);
    }

    private int search(int[] weights, int days, int low, int high) {
        if (low >= high) {
            return low;
        }

        int capacity = low + (high - low) / 2;
        int usedDays = countDays(weights, capacity);

        if (usedDays <= days) {
            return search(weights, days, low, capacity);
        }

        return search(weights, days, capacity + 1, high);
    }

    private int countDays(int[] weights, int capacity) {
        int usedDays = 1;
        int currentLoad = 0;

        for (int weight : weights) {
            if (currentLoad + weight > capacity) {
                usedDays++;
                currentLoad = weight;
            } else {
                currentLoad += weight;
            }
        }

        return usedDays;
    }
}
```

There are `O(log S)` recursive calls, and each call scans all `n` packages, so the time complexity is `O(n log S)`, where `S` is the total weight. The recursion uses `O(log S)` stack space.

Instead of starting the capacity search at only the largest package, this version also considers the average load needed per day. The lower bound becomes `max(heaviest package, total weight / days)`. This does not change correctness, but it can remove some values that are obviously too small.

The check stores the unused load for the current day. If the next package does not fit, it opens the next day with that package. For example, with `[3, 2, 2, 4, 1, 4]`, capacity `6` uses days `[3,2]`, `[2,4]`, and `[1,4]`, so it works for three days.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool fits(vector<int>& weights, int days, int capacity) {
        int usedDays = 1;
        int remaining = capacity;

        for (int weight : weights) {
            if (weight <= remaining) {
                remaining -= weight;
            } else {
                usedDays++;
                remaining = capacity - weight;

                if (usedDays > days) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    int shipWithinDays(vector<int>& weights, int days) {
        int totalWeight = 0;
        int heaviest = 0;

        for (int weight : weights) {
            totalWeight += weight;
            heaviest = max(heaviest, weight);
        }

        int low = max(heaviest, (totalWeight + days - 1) / days);
        int high = totalWeight;

        while (low < high) {
            int middle = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (fits(weights, days, middle)) {
                high = middle;
            } else {
                low = middle + 1;
            }
        }

        return low;
    }
};
```

The array is read only. Binary search makes `O(log S)` checks, each taking `O(n)`, for `O(n log S)` time. The extra space is `O(1)`.

The answer is between `max(weights)` and `sum(weights)`. A capacity below the largest package is impossible, while the total sum can always ship everything in one day.

For each middle value, the helper walks from left to right. When the next package does not fit, it starts a new day. If the capacity works, the right side moves left because a smaller answer may still work.

Dry run for `weights = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]` and `days = 3`: capacity `6` creates loads `6`, `4`, and `5`, so it is valid. The search then checks smaller capacities.

